his formula we know due  to the definition of a block Clause that  any Clause blocked for X has to include  both not Y and Y  which makes any such Clause useless for  shortening a proof  by scaling up versions of this idea we  can impose some restrictions upon the  set of redundant Clauses derivable in  each per system that we have  proving a separation then turns into a  balancing act where we try to limit the  abilities of one system while making  sure not to constrain the other one too  much  let's look at a summary of the results  we proved  here you see some generalizations of BC  minus  and those three  that are closest to it are its weakest  generalizations in qualitatively  different ways  the black arrows are relationships that  are already known the red ones are new  in this work  the way to read this diagram is as  follows  a solid Arrow from P to Q like this  means that P simulates Q  a dashed Arrow from P to Q with a circle  as the arrowhead like this  means that P is separated from Q and A  solid Arrow from P to Q with a gap in  between like this  means that P ball simulates and is  separated from Q hence is strictly  stronger  with variations on the recipe that I've  described in the previous slides we are  able to prove a whole bunch of new  separations  also in ongoing work we have the two  missing relationships here determined  conditionally and we are now working on  proving a lower bound against this SPC  minus system to make those relationships  unconditional and thus give a complete  picture of the relationships between the  systems on this triangle that are the  closest to BC minus  and by the way this SBC minus and spr  minus  are the systems which have those strong  upper bounds for the combinatorial  principles that I showed earlier  now in the rest of the talk I want to  explicitly show the proof of the  separations but I want to emphasize that  they're relatively simple at least  compared to improving separations  completely from scratch 